To achieve this, you can use HTML and CSS to position the SVG icon to the right side of the input field, and JavaScript to handle the click event. Below is an example of how you can do this:

### HTML
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with Icon</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" id="myInput" placeholder="Enter something...">
        <div class="icon-container" id="iconContainer">
            <!-- SVG Icon for Reading Glass -->
            <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                <path d="M12 4C10.89 4 9.99 4.89 9.99 6C9.99 7.11 10.89 8 12 8C13.11 8 14 7.11 14 6C14 4.89 13.11 4 12 4ZM12 14C9.79 14 8 15.79 8 18V20H20V18C20 15.79 18.21 14 16 14H14V12H10V14H8V12C8 9.79 9.79 8 12 8H16C18.21 8 20 9.79 20 12V14H12V14Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
        </div>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### CSS (styles.css)
```css
.input-container {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 300px;
}

.input-container input {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 10px;
    padding-right: 40px; /* Make space for the icon */
    box-sizing: border-box;
}

.icon-container {
    position: absolute;
    right: 10px;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    cursor: pointer;
}

.icon-container svg {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    fill: #000; /* Icon color */
}
```

### JavaScript (script.js)
```javascript
document.getElementById('iconContainer').addEventListener('click', function() {
    const inputValue = document.getElementById('myInput').value;
    if (inputValue) {
        // Call your function here
        console.log('Input value:', inputValue);
        // Example function call
        myFunction(inputValue);
    }
});

function myFunction(value) {
    // Your function logic here
    alert('Input value: ' + value);
}
```

### Explanation
1. **HTML**: The input field and the SVG icon are wrapped in a `div` with the class `input-container`. The SVG icon is placed inside another `div` with the class `icon-container`.
2. **CSS**: The `input-container` is styled to be a relative container. The input field is given padding on the right to make space for the icon. The `icon-container` is positioned absolutely to the right of the input field.
3. **JavaScript**: An event listener is added to the `icon-container` to handle the click event. When the icon is clicked, the value of the input field is retrieved and passed to a function (`myFunction` in this example).

This setup ensures that the SVG icon is clickable and positioned to the right of the input field, and a function is called when the icon is clicked.

